I mean, I was getting wrong, but we had a meeting with Matt over breakfast, and we were like to present our four ideas.
我的意思是，如果我记错了，但我们和马特吃过早餐开会，我们向他展示了我们的四个想法。

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:28.949
And I think the first one we read was how I met your mother, and he was like, that's it.
我想我们读的第一个是《老爸老妈的浪漫史》，他的反应是：就是这个。

00:03:28.949 --> 00:03:29.949
That's the one.
就是这个。

00:03:29.949 --> 00:03:31.229
You can read me the other ones, but that's the one.
你可以把其他的读给我听，但就是这个。

00:03:31.229 --> 00:03:33.189
And so he was kind of like a tip.
所以他有点像是一个暗示。

00:03:33.189 --> 00:03:35.990
Do you remember what the kind of like log light of it was?
你还记得它的大致梗概是什么吗？

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:48.800
I think in light, the sort of storytelling perspective built into the title, and then when me started talking about how personal it would be to us that it would be sort of like the couple that's a little bit like me and my wife and the single guy that's a little bit like Carter at the time.
我想从某种角度来看，标题中内置的那种叙事视角，然后当我们开始谈论这会对我们有多么个人化时，它会有点像那个有点像我和我妻子的情侣，以及当时有点像卡特的单身汉。

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.199
I think he was just like, that's where your heart is, your heart isn't, you know how to write that.
我想他当时的想法是：这就是你们的心之所在，你知道如何去写那个。

00:03:54.199 --> 00:03:57.759
But he had, his reaction was more like visceral and immediate.
但他的反应更发自内心，也更直接。

00:03:57.759 --> 00:03:58.960
It was almost a little more magical than that.
几乎比那更神奇一点。

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.159
The more we talked about it, the more it was like that also was right.
我们谈论得越多，就越觉得这也是对的。

00:04:01.159 --> 00:04:02.639
What you know, do that one.
你知道的，就做那个。

00:04:02.639 --> 00:04:06.879
You don't know the worlds of these other things you're pitching right your world.
你并不了解你推销的这些其他东西的世界，写你自己的世界。

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.800
And we went from New York, we missed New York.
我们从纽约出发，我们想念纽约。

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.479
was part of the flavor of the show or nostalgia for having left what was our home for a bunch of years.
这是节目风格的一部分，或者是对离开多年家的留恋。

00:04:13.479 --> 00:04:15.879
And he's like, you can do that.
他说：你们可以做到这一点。

00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:16.879
That's going to bring out the heart.
那会激发真情实感。

00:04:16.879 --> 00:04:17.879
Right, Carter?
对吧，卡特？

00:04:17.879 --> 00:04:18.879
He was.
他是。

00:04:18.879 --> 00:04:22.680
He's, I mean, he's a softie and he's got a real emotional core that Matt Rice.
我的意思是，他是个感性的人，马特·赖斯有着真正的情感核心。

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.399
And he, I think, you know, he's kind of our age is a little bit older.
我想，他大概和我们年纪相仿，稍微年长一点。

00:04:25.399 --> 00:04:34.160
But like, I think he wanted to see what we could do just writing about because Oliver Bean was about a 12-year-old kid living in the 1960s and then we were writing American dad.
但是，我想他想看看我们纯粹通过写作能做些什么，因为《奥利弗·宾》讲的是一个生活在20世纪60年代的12岁小孩，然后我们在写《美国老爸》。

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:45.119
We were writing all these shows that were like sort of not, not our, we worked on the method in red sitcom, so I mean, the natural choices to have the voice of methamam.
我们在写所有这些不属于我们的节目，我们曾为梅森·雷德情景喜剧工作过，所以我的意思是，最自然的选择是拥有梅瑟曼的声音。

00:04:45.119 --> 00:04:56.143
But yeah, I think he just saw that it was like, oh, this is going to be Craig and Carter really writing their own lives and talking about stuff that's going on with them.
但是，是的，我想他只是看出这将是克雷格和卡特真正书写自己的生活，并讨论他们身上正在发生的事情。

00:04:56.143 --> 00:04:57.439
And that's exciting.
这让人兴奋。

00:04:57.439 --> 00:05:01.299
There's a vulnerability to it that I think was that appealed to us, too.
我觉得其中有一种脆弱感，这也吸引了我们。

00:05:01.299 --> 00:05:08.964
