我的经纪人给我的建议是保持挑剔，你知道的，所以我只会为那些我真正相信的东西争取机会。

00:33:39.766 --> 00:33:46.080
And I believed in your show by the time I got back to the car, my agent called me and said, well, that was quick.
而且我相信你们的节目，当我回到车里时，我的经纪人打电话给我说，哇，这可真快。

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:48.651
They're, they want to do a test deal with you.
他们，他们想跟你谈测试合约。

00:33:48.651 --> 00:33:50.495
They want take you to the studio.
他们想带你去制片公司。

00:33:50.495 --> 00:33:51.948
Your car was 10 feet away.
你的车当时就在10英尺外。

00:33:51.948 --> 00:33:56.083
So then you guys also had a couple more days of seeing other Ted's, right?
所以随后你们还花了几天时间见了其他扮演特德的人，对吧？

00:33:56.083 --> 00:33:59.938
We had to keep seeing other Ted's even though we knew it's so shitty.
我们不得不继续见其他特德，尽管我们心里明知这太烂了。

00:33:59.938 --> 00:34:06.085
I feel bad because like we knew we had you and then other people come in and nobody could be you great actors.
我感觉很糟糕，因为我们明明知道我们已经选定了你，然后其他人走进来，却没有一个人能比得上你，他们都是伟大的演员。

00:34:06.085 --> 00:34:17.708
I mean people came in and we and you need to go into the studio with a few in the network to test like to go into a room with the executives and have you and as it turned out Kobe, do chemistry reads and see.
我是说，有人进来了，我们——你需要带几个人去制片公司和电视台试镜，也就是去和一个房间的高管们见面，让你和——事实证明还有科比，进行默契度试读并看看情况。

00:34:17.708 --> 00:34:23.016
We had to have that happen with a couple of other people, but we knew we were in love with you.
我们不得不和其他几个人经历这个过程，但我们知道我们已经爱上你了。

00:34:23.016 --> 00:34:23.854
All of us were.
我们所有人都是。

00:34:23.854 --> 00:34:29.666
And that's harrowing because we could have been, we could have had a different feeling than the network.
而那真的很煎熬，因为我们当时的感受可能会和电视台不一致。

00:34:29.666 --> 00:34:32.515
The network could have been like, no, it's not him.
电视台可能会觉得，不，不是他。

00:34:32.515 --> 00:34:37.768
And that would have been so heartbreaking, but everybody went after the other went, we get it.
那会让人非常心碎，但每个人在看完其他人后都说，我们懂了。

00:34:37.768 --> 00:34:40.227
And that doesn't always happen either, kids.
孩子们，这可不是每次都会发生的。

00:34:40.227 --> 00:34:41.791
That does not always happen.
这并不总是会发生。

00:34:41.130 --> 00:34:41.630
Yeah.
对。

00:34:41.630 --> 00:34:57.479
Well, I found in show business, and maybe it's like this in other areas of life, but If a job is not yours, the door won't open, and it just simply won't open no matter how right you think you are for it, how much you want it.
嗯，我在演艺圈发现——也许生活中的其他领域也是如此——如果一份工作不属于你，大门就不会为你敞开，而且无论你觉得自己有多合适、多么渴望得到它，它就是不会敞开。

00:34:57.329 --> 00:35:00.889
If the job is yours, the doors keep flying open.
如果这份工作属于你，大门就会不断为你敞开。

00:35:00.889 --> 00:35:13.090
And I've learned to trust effortlessness in my career, like when you click with people and it's easy and fun and you're all just the enthusiasm is really high, you want to make the thing.
我在自己的职业生涯中学会了相信那种毫不费力的顺其自然，比如当你和某些人一拍即合，一切都轻松有趣，大家的积极性都非常高，你就很想把这个东西做出来。

00:35:13.090 --> 00:35:19.010
I don't, I sometimes think effort, like hard grinding effort can be a little overrated.
我不——有时我觉得努力，比如那种死记硬背般的艰难努力，可能有点被高估了。

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:22.090
That you need to pay attention to where things are actually in flow.
你需要关注事情究竟在哪里处于顺流之中。

00:35:22.090 --> 00:35:23.090
Yeah.
对。

00:35:23.090 --> 00:35:25.090
I've been training for a long time.
我训练了很长时间。

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:38.090
And that's cool to hear you say that because from the, and you've now been on both sides of the table having written and directed your own movies and stuff, it is, at the beginning of these sections, you get this list.
听到你这么说真的很酷，因为从——而且你现在自己也写过并导演过电影之类的内容，坐在了桌子的两侧，在这些环节开始的时候，你会拿到这样一份名单。

00:35:38.090 --> 00:35:46.054
And here's who we're seeing today, and it's like a list and it's like a name and then little space for you to write your comments.
这就是我们今天要见的人，就像是一份名单，上面有一个名字，旁边留有一小块空白让你写评论。

00:35:46.054 --> 00:35:50.403
And so it's just like a very clean sheet of paper with like nine names.
所以这就像是一张非常干净的纸，上面写着九个名字。

00:35:50.403 --> 00:35:52.057
And you just like see that.
而你只需要看着它。

00:35:52.057 --> 00:36:03.697
You think like these are like nine people right now just hoping that that they're like and and and it's by the way it's this like nine different branches of like where the universe could go.
你会想，这就像是此时此刻有九个人正怀揣着希望，希望他们能被选中，而且顺便说一句，这就像是命运可能会走向的九个不同分支。

00:36:03.697 --> 00:36:27.099
This could be a show with this person as Ted and this person as Ted and and it's it's and I feel like there's it's it's a lot of the stuff we talked about on on how I'm in your mother, because just the fact that destiny takes such a big part in the search for love, and how I'm in your mother at its core is this struggle between is falling in love something that I go out and seek.
这可能会是一部由这个人扮演特德的剧，或者由那个人扮演特德，而且它——我觉得这其中有很多我们在《老爸老妈浪漫史》中探讨过的内容，因为命运在寻爱之旅中占据了如此重要的比重，而《老爸老妈浪漫史》的核心就是这样一种挣扎：坠入爱河是我主动出去寻找的东西吗？

00:36:27.099 --> 00:36:32.780
Do I take action to meet the person that I spend the rest of my life with, or is it something that happens to me?
我是不是应该采取行动去遇见那个将与我共度余生的人，还是说这是某种降临在我身上的事情？

00:36:32.780 --> 00:36:36.300
I have to just sit back and let it happen, and that's the casting process.
我只能坐享其成、顺其自然，而这就是选角的过程。

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:40.699
It really is like, I feel like there's no way to answer to that.
真的就是这样，我觉得这个问题根本无解。

00:36:40.699 --> 00:36:42.019
It's an unsolvable question.
这是一个无法解答的问题。

00:36:42.019 --> 00:36:49.500
There's a good theme has emerged of our conversation of just like, there's some preparation and then there's some luck in letting you and leaning into that part too.
我们的对话中浮现出了一个很好的主题：既需要一些准备，也需要一些运气，并且去顺应那部分运气。

00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:50.699
And your right touch and writing too.
正如你刚才在写作时提到的那样。

00:36:50.699 --> 00:36:55.340
It's like, you can't, if you're forcing it too hard, that might be a sign that it's not quite right.
这就好比，你不能，如果你用力过猛，那可能就是一个迹象，表明它其实并不那么合适。

00:36:55.340 --> 00:36:57.139
And the doors did keep opening for you.
而大门确实一直在为你敞开。

00:36:57.139 --> 00:37:04.360
In some ways, you guys had to honor the themes of your show and the creation of your show.
从某种程度上说，你们必须尊重你们节目的主题以及你们节目的创作。

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:05.360
Yeah.
对。

00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:12.119
I'm saying you had to be open to the right person at the right time.
我是说你们必须对在正确的时间遇到对的人保持开放的心态。

00:37:12.119 --> 00:37:18.480
So I'm trying to remember, so I go to the studio, you had one other guy there, I think another 10.
让我想想，我去制片公司的时候，那里还有另外一个人，我想是另一个“特德”。

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:19.480
Yeah.
对。

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:20.480
The other guy was Bob Saggett.
另一个人是鲍勃·萨吉特。

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:22.480
He was a little too old for the role.
他演这个角色稍微有点老了。

00:37:22.480 --> 00:37:24.480
That's why he became a future 10.
这就是为什么他后来成了未来的特德。

00:37:24.480 --> 00:37:25.480
A consolation prize.
一个安慰奖。

00:37:25.480 --> 00:37:26.480
Yeah.
对。

00:37:26.480 --> 00:37:27.480
Yeah.
对。

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:28.480
He was really good.
他真的很棒。

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:30.480
But it's like, here's a little old.
但这就好比，他稍微有点老了。

00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:31.480
But no, no, no.
但是不，不，不。

00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:33.480
I don't think we should say names before that.
我觉得我们在这之前不该说出名字。

00:37:33.480 --> 00:37:34.480
No, no, no.
不，不，不。

00:37:34.480 --> 00:37:35.480
That's not why.
原因不是这个。

00:37:35.480 --> 00:37:37.719
But very talented, another very talented actor.
但他很有才华，另一个非常有才华的演员。

00:37:37.719 --> 00:37:49.079
I'm just going to throw this in, I remember being there, because you go to these things, and there's two, there's always two, and everyone in the room, including the head executives at CBS.
我只是想插一句，我记得当时在那儿，因为你去参加这些试镜，通常会有两个，总是有两个，屋里的每个人，包括CBS的高层主管。

00:37:49.079 --> 00:37:56.679
Everybody knows there's the guy that they want, and then there's the other guy that they're bringing in just to show like, yeah, but we really want this guy.
大家都知道有一个他们内定的人，然后还有另一个他们找来陪跑的人，好显得——没错，但我们确实想要这个。

00:37:56.679 --> 00:38:08.689
And I think everyone knew that, including both of the actors involved, and I remember feeling just because my, my, my mind always says like, how can I, how can I have more anxiety about this moment?
我觉得大家都心知肚明，包括涉事的两个演员在内，我记得当时的感觉是，因为我的脑回路总是会想：我该怎么做才能为这一刻产生更多的焦虑呢？

00:38:08.689 --> 00:38:09.189
Okay.
好吧。

00:38:09.189 --> 00:38:10.827
I'll feel bad for this guy.
我会为这家伙感到难过的。

00:38:10.827 --> 00:38:19.197
And, and I remember because he was really, and, and I'm not to say who he was, but he went on to be super funny and great on another show.
而且，我记得因为他真的——我不是说他是谁，但他后来在另一部剧里变得超级搞笑而且表现很棒。

00:38:19.197 --> 00:38:22.048
That, that, that was, that I was a huge fan of.
那部剧，我曾经是个超级粉丝。

00:38:22.048 --> 00:38:25.930
So, yeah, it was, that was, it was, it was, it worked out right.
所以，是的，结果确实是，挺圆满的。

00:38:25.930 --> 00:38:27.386
It was nice to see that.
看到那样的结果挺好的。

00:38:27.386 --> 00:38:31.146
Can I say the, I do know this story that really made me laugh.
我能说那个吗，我确实知道这个故事，当时把我笑死。

00:38:31.146 --> 00:38:35.756
It's a Rob Greenberg line because I think I also had to read a Barney scene.
这是罗布·格林伯格的台词，因为我想我也得试读一段巴尼的戏。

00:38:35.756 --> 00:38:40.730
It was it was Barney trying to get me to go to laser tag or something in the test.
那是巴尼在试镜时试图拉我去打激光枪战之类的。

00:38:40.730 --> 00:38:49.039
And Rob Greenberg apparently said afterwards about the other guy, I feel like he wants to go to laser tag, like he would go to laser tag.
罗布·格林伯格事后显然对另一个演员评价说，我觉得他是真的想去打激光枪战，就像他平时会去打的那种。

00:38:49.039 --> 00:38:52.618
I mean, Josh did not want to go to laser tag with this guy.
我的意思是，乔什才不想跟这家伙去打激光枪战呢。

00:38:52.618 --> 00:38:53.118
Yeah.
是啊。

00:38:53.118 --> 00:38:53.618
Yeah.
是啊。

00:38:53.618 --> 00:38:54.118
Yeah.
是啊。

00:38:54.118 --> 00:38:57.575
They're like a little jockier, a little different energy.
他们看起来更有点运动男孩的感觉，能量场有点不一样。

00:38:57.575 --> 00:38:58.075
Yeah.
是啊。

00:38:58.075 --> 00:38:58.575
Yeah.
是啊。

00:38:58.575 --> 00:38:59.075
Yeah.
是啊。

00:38:59.075 --> 00:39:22.367
It had to be plausible that you would not have gone and you would not have I'm just curious, when people become so important and kind of iconic in your life, there's that moment when you see them for the first time that kind of gets etched in your own personal history, I'm just curious how you envisioned Robin Shurbatsky, I don't know if that was her last name in the pilot, was it?
必须得合乎情理，让你觉得你不会去，而且你也——我只是好奇，当人们在你生命中变得如此重要且具有某种标志性时，你第一次见到他们的那个瞬间，会被铭刻在你的个人历史中，我只是好奇你当时是怎么构想罗宾·什巴茨基的，我不知道试播集里那是她的姓吗，是吗？

00:39:20.989 --> 00:39:21.989
I think you came up with that.
我想那是你想出来的。

00:39:21.989 --> 00:39:22.989
Yeah, it was.
对，就是那样。

00:39:22.989 --> 00:39:23.489
I was.
我是。

00:39:23.489 --> 00:39:28.154
It was a sure Robin kiddie Sherbatsky is a character from Anna Karenina.
罗宾·凯蒂·什巴茨基是《安娜·卡列尼娜》里的一个角色。

00:39:28.154 --> 00:39:38.130
I think I like I think I was like thought I was fainting myself a reader and I was like I'm going to read Anna Karenina and and got like 20 pages into it.
我想我当时——觉得自己好像在假装是个爱书之人，我想“我要读《安娜·卡列尼娜》”，然后读了大约20页。

00:39:38.130 --> 00:39:40.138
And then you got what you need.
然后你就得到了你需要的东西。

00:39:40.138 --> 00:39:41.563
You got what you need.
你得到了你需要的东西。

00:39:41.563 --> 00:39:42.340
Even though.
尽管。

00:39:42.340 --> 00:39:42.840
Yeah.
对。

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:43.359
Exactly.
没错。

00:39:43.359 --> 00:39:45.172
So you told story I got you.
所以你讲了这个故事，我懂了。

00:39:45.172 --> 00:39:48.994
Did you have an idea and you're kind of who this woman was?
你当时对这个女人是谁有个大致的构想吗？

00:39:48.994 --> 00:39:52.687
It's interesting because you've got five main characters.
这很有趣，因为你有五个主要角色。

00:39:52.400 --> 00:40:08.320
There's four of them were absolutely drawn from real life, like Ted Marshall and Lily were me, Craig and Rebecca, you know, and again, then other writers come in and like add their own, hang their own stuff on the Christmas tree.
其中有四个绝对是取材于现实生活，比如泰德、马歇尔和莉莉就是我、克雷格和丽贝卡，你知道的，当然，后来其他编剧加入进来，会把他们自己的东西挂到这棵圣诞树上。

00:40:08.320 --> 00:40:18.320
But and Barney was very much inspired by a couple guys that sort of wore that kind of false staffian presence in our lives when we lived in New York.
但是，巴尼很大程度上是受到我们在纽约生活时，身边那些带有那种虚张声势气息的家伙的启发。

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:31.800
The only character who was completely made up whole cloth was Robin, and so it really was like the casting process was sort of this process of like, let's see if we can find her.
唯一一个完全凭空捏造的角色就是罗宾，所以选角过程真的就像是一场寻找她的过程。

00:40:31.800 --> 00:40:32.800
Let's see who this could be.
看看这会是谁。

00:40:32.800 --> 00:40:49.099
It really was like, it wasn't like, oh yeah, I mean, having those, I think Neil got cast as Barney despite the fact that he sort of was physically nothing like the people that Barney He was, was based on but like that, you know, I remember you guys tossing around Jack Black with a prototype.
真的就是这样，并不是说，哦，对，我的意思是，拥有那些——我觉得尼尔被选中饰演巴尼，尽管他的外在体型和巴尼原本的蓝本完全不像，但是，你知道的，我记得你们曾讨论过杰克·布莱克作为候选原型。

00:40:49.099 --> 00:40:54.079
Like, yeah, I think you saw him much more like, yeah, that's that road that was bigger and, yeah.
就像，对，我觉得你当时看他的路线更偏向于——对，那种体型更壮实、并且，对。

00:40:54.079 --> 00:40:55.079
Yeah.
是啊。

00:40:55.079 --> 00:41:03.320
There was, there was like a Vince Vaughn road and like a Jack Black road, but we were more down the Jack Black road, I think that wouldn't you say Carter and then like Neil came in and it was like, shit.
当时有，当时有一条文斯·沃恩的路线和一条杰克·布莱克的路线，但我们更倾向于杰克·布莱克的路线，我觉得卡特，你难道不这么认为吗？然后尼尔来了，就觉得——靠。

00:41:03.320 --> 00:41:04.320
That's good.
这太棒了。

00:41:04.320 --> 00:41:05.320
It's too good.
好得过分了。

00:41:05.320 --> 00:41:14.084
But I do think the one, the one insight that I think we, we happened upon that I'm proud of us for happening upon was this feeling, because, you know, of course, you get picked up.
不过我觉得我们想到的那一个、让我为之自豪的洞察，其实就是这种感觉，因为你知道，节目当然会被买下。

00:41:14.084 --> 00:41:19.440
And of course, the first thing is like, who can we get in it that will help its chances of getting on the air?
当然，第一件事就是，我们能找谁来演，才能提高它播出的几率？

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:20.440
Who's famous?
谁是明星？

00:41:20.440 --> 00:41:29.823
But there was a feeling like, I remember thinking like so much of Robin is built into this moment where Ted looks across the crowded bar and sees this person.
但当时有一种感觉，我记得当时想的是，罗宾身上的很多特质都融入了这一刻：泰德隔着拥挤的酒吧看过去，看到了这个人。

00:41:29.823 --> 00:41:34.099
And we see here for the first time as Ted seeing her for the first time.
我们第一次看到她，也就是泰德第一次看到她。

00:41:34.099 --> 00:41:35.099
Yeah.
对。

00:41:35.099 --> 00:41:39.539
And if it's someone that you already have this built in expectation of, of like, oh, I I love to ruin this, right?
如果这个人是你已经带有一种预设期待的，比如，哦，我真想毁了她，对吧？

00:41:39.539 --> 00:41:40.539
I hate it.
我讨厌这样。

00:41:40.539 --> 00:41:45.590
And that like you're already, you're outside of the moment and you're not having that.
这就好像你已经置身于那个时刻之外，无法体会那种感觉。

00:41:45.590 --> 00:41:49.630
It's not that that moment of like, there's a mystery here to be solved.
那不是那种“这里有一个谜团等待解开”的时刻。

00:41:49.630 --> 00:41:55.630
I mean, I want to get to know this person, but it can't be like you see, you see a person and you're like, I know what you did last summer.
我的意思是，我想了解这个人，但这不能像你看、看到一个人然后心想“我知道你去年夏天干了什么”。

00:41:55.630 --> 00:41:56.630
I can't.
我做不到。

00:41:56.630 --> 00:41:57.630
Yeah.
对。

00:41:57.630 --> 00:41:58.630
Okay.
好吧。

00:41:58.630 --> 00:42:00.630
It has to be like I have to know what they did last summer.
感觉应该像是，我必须知道他们去年夏天干了什么。

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:04.510
You have to not know what they did any summer because tell me about your summer.
你一定不能知道他们哪个夏天干了什么，因为跟我讲讲你的夏天吧。

00:42:04.510 --> 00:42:10.434
I think a mystery about me and Kobe's relative obscurity or anonymity at that point really served us.
我觉得我和蔻碧当时相对不为人知或默默无闻，真的帮了我们大忙。

00:42:10.434 --> 00:42:16.594
It served you guys in the story because you're, you're discovering these people, you're discovering them.
这在故事里对你们很有帮助，因为你们在发掘这些人，你们在认识他们。

00:42:16.594 --> 00:42:24.219
And you want to feel like you, you've just happened upon them rather than having any, you know, sort of foreign knowledge of them.
而且你希望感觉自己是偶然碰到了他们，而不是对他们有什么先入为主的了解。

00:42:24.219 --> 00:42:29.558
Yeah, it was perfect who was more famous and who was not more famous in that of those five.
对，这太完美了，那五个人里谁更有名，谁没那么有名。

00:42:29.558 --> 00:42:30.789
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:42:30.789 --> 00:42:34.933
like the perfect, the famous people fell into the right spots in the cast.
就像完美地，那些大明星都在演员阵容里落在了对的位置上。

00:42:34.933 --> 00:42:36.332
And, and, and we met you.
而且，而且，我们遇到了你。

00:42:36.332 --> 00:42:38.236
It's the word met is in the title.
标题里就带有“遇到”这个词。

00:42:38.236 --> 00:42:39.692
It's about meeting people.
这就是关于遇见人。

00:42:39.692 --> 00:42:40.363
We meet Ted.
我们遇到了泰德。

00:42:40.363 --> 00:42:42.491
We never, we've never seen you before.
我们以前从没见过你。

00:42:42.491 --> 00:42:43.275
We meet Robin.
我们遇到了罗宾。

00:42:43.275 --> 00:42:44.562
And that, you're right.
而且，你说得对。

00:42:44.562 --> 00:42:46.690
It was like the audience met you guys.
就好像观众遇到了你们一样。

00:42:46.690 --> 00:42:56.544
But Carter, it also occurs to me when you're, when you're talking about Robin being invented out of whole cloth on some level, she was the woman you were hoping to meet, right?
但卡特，我还想到，当你在谈论罗宾是在某种程度上凭空捏造出来的时候，她就是你希望遇到的那个女人，对吧？

00:42:56.544 --> 00:43:00.799
Like she was, because she became dimensional and spiky and all these things.
就像她是，因为她变得立体、带刺而且拥有所有这些特质。

00:43:00.799 --> 00:43:06.510
But she was like, what if I looked across the bar and just spotted this woman, what would she be like?
但她的感觉就像是，如果我朝酒吧那边看去，恰好发现了这个女人，她会是什么样的人？

00:43:06.510 --> 00:43:07.510
For sure.
肯定的。

00:43:07.510 --> 00:43:08.510
Yeah.
对。

00:43:08.510 --> 00:43:10.139
She was built that way.
她就是按这个路子塑造的。

00:43:10.139 --> 00:43:29.199
But it was a combination of like, make her everything that would be like, of course, he falls in love with this person, but also give her things that it He was almost like, you know, it's in sort of like judoing the, like, what's the tension of this series going to be?
但这是一种结合，既要让她具备一切“当然啦，他会爱上这个人”的特质，也要赋予她一些……这几乎就像是，你知道的，有点像借力打力，思考这个系列的冲突会是什么？

00:43:29.199 --> 00:43:35.960
Like we've got this character, this who just wants to, you know, his drive is fallen love.
比如我们有这样一个角色，他一心只想，你知道的，他的驱动力就是坠入爱河。

00:43:35.960 --> 00:43:41.079
He has this, like, he gets hit by lightning in this first episode of, like, Urika, I want to get married.
他在第一集里就像被雷击中一样，有了一种“尤里卡”般的顿悟，我想结婚。

00:43:41.079 --> 00:43:42.079
I'm like, ready.
我准备好了。

00:43:42.079 --> 00:43:43.079
Okay.
好吧。

00:43:43.079 --> 00:43:44.079
My friend just got engaged.
我的朋友刚订婚。

00:43:44.079 --> 00:43:45.559
I want to settle down.
我想安顿下来。

00:43:45.559 --> 00:43:47.639
Like, how do we create tension?
那么，我们该如何制造戏剧冲突呢？

00:43:47.639 --> 00:43:57.929
And I feel like that sort of like naturally led to Robin just becoming this character that her central drive, at least at the beginning, is I don't want to settle that.
我觉得这自然而然地让罗宾成为了这样一个角色：她的核心驱动力，至少在一开始是，我可不想安顿下来。

00:43:57.929 --> 00:44:16.440
But I do remember my first seeing Kobe for the first time, down a hallway, walking towards me, and you know, you just have to do all this, you know, chit chat, and then you are supposed to simulate this thing together.
不过我确实记得我第一次见到蔻碧的情形，在一条走廊里，她朝我走来，你知道的，你们得做所有这些寒暄，然后去模拟演出这个情境。

00:44:16.440 --> 00:44:23.639
But I do remember at I think the network test, so it was just me by the time, I think you just brought me to the network, is that right?
但我确实记得，在电视台试镜的时候，当时只有我一个人，我想你只是把我带到了电视台，是这样吗？

00:44:23.639 --> 00:44:24.480
Or did you bring both guys?
还是说你把他们两个都带来了？

00:44:24.480 --> 00:44:25.559
God, Carter, do you remember?
天哪，卡特，你还记得吗？

00:44:25.559 --> 00:44:28.639
I feel like in the network, you have to still bring two people.
我觉得去电视台试镜的话，你通常还是得带两个人。

00:44:28.639 --> 00:44:33.199
I feel like it's always, there's always another person I can't remember, but maybe it was just you at that point.
我觉得好像总是，总会有另一个人我想不起来了，但也许那时候就只有你。

00:44:33.199 --> 00:44:37.280
I mean, because when you were with Kobe in a scene, that was the network, I think.
我是说，因为当你和科比（Kobe）一起拍一个场景时，那是电视台，我想。

00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:38.559
And that was probably just you guys.
那可能就只有你们俩。

00:44:38.559 --> 00:44:39.559
Oh, yeah, that was okay.
哦，对，那还行。

00:44:39.559 --> 00:44:43.360
So maybe the studio, that's the last step for you are officially hired.
所以也许电视台，那是你正式被录用的最后一步。

00:44:43.360 --> 00:44:44.360
Yeah.
对。

00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:52.559
But I do remember I had gotten, I kind of would would get these waves of heroic confidence.
但我确实记得我当时，我会有那种突如其来的英雄般的自信。

00:44:52.559 --> 00:45:16.472
Like the higher stakes at God, I would get like a little more like, I'm going to get this, you know, like it was my, this competitive street came out in me and I would sometimes say things that were like a little, like just a little like Hutzpahy in a way that was like risky, but I remember when Kobe, she threw an actual drink in my face.
就像赌注越高，我就越会觉得，我一定能拿下，你知道的，我骨子里的那种好胜心被激发出来了，我有时会说一些听起来有点……有点像是在耍小聪明的话，虽然有点冒险，但我记得当科比直接把一杯饮料泼在我脸上时。

00:45:16.472 --> 00:45:21.888
She had some water, and she threw the water in my face, and everyone laughed.
她当时手里有点水，然后把水泼在我脸上，大家都笑了。

00:45:21.888 --> 00:45:24.350
And I, it seems over at that point.
而我，当时觉得一切都完了。

00:45:24.349 --> 00:45:32.090
And I turned to the brass at CBS in 20th, and I said, as if this process weren't humiliating enough.
我转过身对CBS和20世纪福克斯的高层说，就好像这个过程还不够屈辱似的。

00:45:32.090 --> 00:45:42.300
And it got, you know, it got this like really big laugh and I felt, um, I felt like that was good.
然后，你知道的，这引来了一阵非常大的笑声，我觉得，嗯，我觉得这挺好的。

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:51.980
You know, I just wanted to know like, I just wanted that I, I, I, I always hated the power imbalance in being an actor and saying like, please give me this role.
你知道，我只是想表达，我只是想……我一直很讨厌作为演员时那种权力的不对等，总是说，请把这个角色给我吧。

00:45:51.980 --> 00:45:55.539
And I always wanted them to know like, I got something going on here.
我一直想让他们知道，我这里也是有东西的。

00:45:55.539 --> 00:45:56.539
Yeah.
对。

00:45:56.539 --> 00:45:57.539
I hope you understand.
我希望你们能明白。

00:45:57.539 --> 00:46:02.179
So I, I just wanted to level it the tiniest bit because it's so, it's so outside.
所以我只是想稍微把天平拉平一点，因为当你走进去的时候。

00:46:02.179 --> 00:46:05.211
the power when you walk in there.
那种权力差距实在是太悬殊了。

00:46:05.211 --> 00:46:09.619
And there are these executives that run empires.
那些高管掌控着整个帝国。

00:46:09.619 --> 00:46:12.957
Seated in like bleacher level, like theater seating, like tiered seating.
他们坐在看台那种层级的座位上，就像剧院阶梯教室那样的阶梯座位。

00:46:12.957 --> 00:46:15.334
Like it's a little fee, a little play just for that.
就像是一场专为他们表演的小型戏剧。

00:46:15.334 --> 00:46:17.619
The whole thing stage managed to make you nervous.
整个过程的舞台调度就是为了让你感到紧张。

00:46:17.619 --> 00:46:18.739
It's not comfortable.
这并不舒服。

00:46:18.739 --> 00:46:19.806
It's very stressful.
压力非常大。

00:46:19.806 --> 00:46:20.873
And it's on purpose.
而且这是故意的。

00:46:20.873 --> 00:46:24.979
They want to see that you can rise to like be like really like under the gun.
他们想看看你是否能在压力下脱颖而出。

00:46:24.979 --> 00:46:27.379
And there's pressure in the air in that room.
那个房间里弥漫着压力。

00:46:27.380 --> 00:46:30.660
And Carter and I of course feel like total frauds.
而卡特和我当然觉得自己彻头彻尾就是个骗子。

00:46:30.660 --> 00:46:44.374
We're sitting in these bleachers with all these executives, meanwhile two months earlier, were the people, you know, bringing our little script to them, like, please, you know, we were the you a month earlier.
我们和所有这些高管一起坐在看台上，而就在两个月前，我们还是拿着小剧本去找他们的人，满脸堆着求求你们，你知道的，我们一个月前还是那个你们。

00:46:44.374 --> 00:46:46.605
And now we're in the judgey seats.
而现在我们坐在了评判席上。

00:46:46.605 --> 00:46:55.398
And so like I felt I had such empathy for you in that moment, but you were great and human Kobe, the energy of human Kobe, undeniable.
所以我当时对你充满了同情，但你表现得很棒，而且充满了人情味，科比，那种充满人情味的能量是无法否认的。

00:46:55.398 --> 00:47:08.259
I did feel loved and supported by you guys, by Pam, by Rob Greenberg, by Megan and Dylan, like I felt like the people that I had established this reports in that first room were really on my side.
我的确感受到了你们的爱和支持，帕姆、罗布·格林伯格（Rob Greenberg）、梅根和迪伦，我觉得我在第一个房间里和他们建立起默契的人是真的站在我这一边的。

00:47:08.259 --> 00:47:11.343
So I felt really, I felt some wind at my sales.
所以我真的觉得，我的风帆借到了东风。

00:47:11.343 --> 00:47:18.954
But I remember, I can't remember if we got the offer or it might have been a Friday maybe had to wait till a Monday.
但我记得，我不记得我们是什么时候拿到offer的，可能是一个星期五，也许得等到星期一。

00:47:18.954 --> 00:47:20.070
I can't remember.
记不清了。

00:47:20.070 --> 00:47:25.516
I think it was, I don't think it was immediate, but I got the job like within days.
我觉得是，我觉得不是马上，但我在几天内就得到了这份工作。

00:47:25.516 --> 00:47:30.044
And then next year, you know, we're making a podcast about this this.
然后第二年，你知道的，我们就要为此做一个播客了。

00:47:30.044 --> 00:47:39.033
Do you guys know the West Memphis three, the three kids who were railroaded into, they were on death row for killing this, I think, girl.
大家知道“西孟菲斯三人组”吗，那三个被冤枉入狱的孩子，他们曾因谋杀一个女孩而被判死刑，我想是这样。

00:47:39.033 --> 00:47:41.461
It was kind of a satanic panic thing.
这有点像那类撒旦恐慌事件。

00:47:41.461 --> 00:47:47.169
And they were called the West Memphis three and they got put on death row as teenagers.
他们被称为“西孟菲斯三人组”，在十几岁时就被判处了死刑。

00:47:47.170 --> 00:47:49.556
And they were, they were totally innocent.
而且他们，他们是完全无辜的。

00:47:49.556 --> 00:47:51.204
It was like an absolute sham.
这简直是一场绝对的骗局。

00:47:51.204 --> 00:47:54.272
But I think they were on death row for about 20 years.
但我想他们在死刑犯监狱里呆了大约20年。

00:47:54.272 --> 00:48:13.080
And there was this guy, Damian Eccles, who I ended up doing an evening at Joe's pub with this kind of evening of written letters and I met him backstage and he'd been on death row for 20 years and I introduced myself to him because I had just seen a documentary about them and he said, I hope it's okay to say this, I'm such a fan.
有个叫达米安·埃科尔斯（Damien Eccles）的人，我后来在乔的酒吧（Joe's Pub）和他一起度过了一个晚上，那种朗读信件的活动，我在后台遇到了他，他在死刑犯监狱里呆了20年，我向他做了自我介绍，因为我刚看过一部关于他们的纪录片，他说，如果我说这个没关系的话，我是你的超级粉丝。

00:48:13.079 --> 00:48:16.280
I watched so much how I met your mother when I was behind bars.
我被关在铁窗后时看了好多《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:48:16.280 --> 00:48:17.280
Oh my God.
哦，天哪。

00:48:17.280 --> 00:48:18.280
Oh my God.
哦，天哪。

00:48:18.280 --> 00:48:19.280
I've never heard that from him.
我从来没听他这么说过。

00:48:19.280 --> 00:48:23.199
And I said, can I ask you, like, what was it about the show?
我说，我能问你吗，这节目到底有什么吸引力？

00:48:23.199 --> 00:48:24.199
I'm always so curious.
我总是很好奇。

00:48:24.199 --> 00:48:39.347
wasn't about the show that that you wanted to see and he said, he said it was fake enough that it took me away from where I was, but it was real enough that it promised a world that I was hoping I could rejoin one day.
到底是什么样的节目内容，让你那么想看？他说，他说这节目虚构得足够抽离，能把我从现实处境中带走，但它又真实得足以给人一种承诺，让我希望有一天能重新融入那个世界。

00:48:39.347 --> 00:48:39.847
Wow.
哇。

00:48:39.847 --> 00:48:40.347
Wow.
哇。

00:48:40.347 --> 00:48:41.390
That's amazing.
太惊人了。

00:48:41.390 --> 00:48:49.936
And I just that always stayed with me that kind of like hyper real but real kind of quality and what that can do to people.
这件事一直留在我的记忆里，那种超现实但又真实的品质，以及它能对人们产生的影响。

00:48:49.936 --> 00:49:00.637
You can look at the show as diversion from your troubles, or you can look at the show as a meta-commentary on your real troubles in your real life, right?
你可以把这个节目看作是对烦恼的逃避，或者你也可以把它看作是对你现实生活中真正烦恼的一种元评论，对吧？

00:49:00.637 --> 00:49:14.186
It's got this ability to meet people where they are, that part of what we're doing, Craig and I keep talking about this mystery that we're trying to solve is like, why does how I met your mother?
它有一种能力，能够在人们身处逆境时与他们产生共鸣，这也是我们一直在探讨的、我和克赖顿（Craig）一直试图解开的谜团的一部分：为什么《老爸老妈浪漫史》……

00:49:14.186 --> 00:49:15.924
Why is it lasted so long?
为什么它能播这么久？

00:49:15.480 --> 00:49:18.465
Why do new generations keep discovering it?
为什么新一代的观众一直在发现它？

00:49:18.465 --> 00:49:30.682
And why does it seem to be medicine, like soul medicine for people in hard times, but you know, that story about Damien is like I think really illustrates something about that.
为什么对于处境艰难的人来说，它似乎成了一种良药，一种心灵良药？关于达米安的那个故事，我觉得真的很好地说明了这一点。

00:49:30.682 --> 00:49:38.110
That's one of my favorite reviews of the show I've ever or assessments of the show I think I've ever heard.
那是我听过的对这部剧最喜欢的评论或评价之一。

00:49:38.110 --> 00:49:39.707
That's beautiful, yeah.
太美妙了，是的。

00:49:39.707 --> 00:49:47.482
There is so much more to our talk with Carter and you'll be able to hear it soon in a bonus episode of the show.
我们与卡特的访谈还有很多内容，你很快就能在该节目的特别单集里听到。

00:49:47.482 --> 00:49:53.313
So look out for how Craig met Carter, if you want to delve into that sorted history.
所以，如果你想深入了解那段错综复杂的历史，请关注《克赖顿是如何遇到卡特的》。

00:49:53.313 --> 00:49:54.424
It's not sorted.
那不叫错综复杂。

00:49:54.424 --> 00:49:55.535
It's delightful.
那是令人愉快的。

